# systemd and the Linux packages

The `.deb` and `.rpm` on the [releases page](https://github.com/{{ github_org }}/{{ project_name }}/releases) install the server as a systemd service. [Installation](../get-started/installation.md#linux-packages) has the install commands; this page is what comes after. The unit confines the process, which is defence in depth: authentication stays the boundary, and the [security model](../security-model.md) says what an authenticated caller then reaches.

## What the package installed

| Path | What it is | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| `/opt/{{ project_name }}/venv` | The server, installed from PyPI at the package's own version (a release candidate installs the wheel attached to its GitHub release) | root |
| `/usr/lib/systemd/system/{{ project_name }}.service` | The unit | root |
| `/etc/{{ project_name }}/env.example` | Every variable, commented out, generated from the server's configuration surface; replaced by an upgrade | root |
| `/etc/{{ project_name }}/env` | Your configuration, copied from `env.example` on first install, readable by root only (`600`), never touched by an upgrade | root |
| `/var/lib/{{ project_name }}` | The state directory, the one path the service may write | `{{ project_name }}:{{ project_name }}` |

The package also creates the `{{ project_name }}` system user and group the service runs as, with `/var/lib/{{ project_name }}` as home and no login shell.

## Configure

Edit `/etc/{{ project_name }}/env`. It holds deviations from the defaults only: every line arrives commented out, and the server starts on its defaults when nothing is set. Remove the `#` from the lines you change. The unit reads the file at start (`EnvironmentFile=`), so an edit needs a restart to take effect. Secrets belong here and nowhere else; the file is already mode `600`.

```ini { .config data-expect="server_name='notes'" }
# /etc/{{ project_name }}/env
{{ env_prefix }}_SERVER_NAME=notes
{{ env_prefix }}_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
```

The unit starts `{{ project_name }} serve --transport http`, so the server listens on `{{ env_prefix }}_HOST` and `{{ env_prefix }}_PORT`, `127.0.0.1:8000` unless set. To serve other machines, configure [authentication](authentication.md) first and put a [reverse proxy](reverse-proxy.md) in front; the [configuration reference](../reference/configuration.md) lists every variable.

## Start and watch

The package installs the unit but does not enable it; starting on boot is your decision:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable --now {{ project_name }}
systemctl status {{ project_name }}
journalctl -u {{ project_name }} -f
sudo systemctl restart {{ project_name }}    # after editing the env file
```

Under journald each record is one JSON object: the unit sets no log format, so the server picks its JSON renderer because journald is not a terminal. `{{ env_prefix }}_LOG_FORMAT=rich` in the env file switches to the coloured renderer. `Restart=on-failure` brings a crashed server back after five seconds.

## What the unit allows

The unit runs the server as the `{{ project_name }}` user with these directives, among others:

| Directive | Effect |
|---|---|
| `ProtectSystem=strict`, `ProtectHome=yes`, `PrivateTmp=yes` | The filesystem is read-only except the paths listed below; `/home`, `/root` and `/run/user` are not visible; a private `/tmp` |
| `StateDirectory={{ project_name }}`, `ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/{{ project_name }}` | The one writable path, created and owned for the service |
| `NoNewPrivileges=yes`, an empty `CapabilityBoundingSet=` | No privilege escalation and no capabilities |
| `PrivateDevices=yes`, `RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6` | No devices; only Unix, IPv4 and IPv6 sockets, so outbound connections to other services work |
| `SystemCallFilter=@system-service` | Only the system calls an ordinary service needs |
| `MemoryDenyWriteExecute=no` | Left open because Python needs writable, executable memory |
| `UMask=0027` | New files are group-readable, so an administrator in the `{{ project_name }}` group can inspect the state directory |

To let the server read or write a path outside `/var/lib/{{ project_name }}`, add a drop-in rather than editing the unit, which the next package upgrade replaces:

```bash
sudo systemctl edit {{ project_name }}
```

```ini
[Service]
ReadWritePaths=/srv/{{ project_name }}
```

`systemctl edit` reloads the unit definitions; restart the service afterwards. The other directives stay as they are. On a host with SELinux enforcing, the directory also needs a label the service may use (`semanage fcontext`, then `restorecon`).

## Upgrade

Install the new package the same way as the first one. Its `postinstall` installs the new version into the existing virtual environment and restarts the service when it is running. `/etc/{{ project_name }}/env` is left alone; `env.example` is replaced, so compare the two for variables the release added:

```bash
diff /etc/{{ project_name }}/env.example /etc/{{ project_name }}/env
```

What a release changes for your clients and your data is on the [Upgrade](../upgrade/index.md) page.

## Without a package

On a distribution the packages do not cover, mirror what the package does. The unit and the environment template live in the repository's `packaging/` directory at every release tag:

```bash
sudo groupadd --system {{ project_name }}
sudo useradd --system --gid {{ project_name }} --no-create-home \
  --home-dir /var/lib/{{ project_name }} --shell /usr/sbin/nologin {{ project_name }}
sudo mkdir -p /opt/{{ project_name }} /etc/{{ project_name }} /var/lib/{{ project_name }}
sudo python3 -m venv /opt/{{ project_name }}/venv
EXTRAS=""  # the value packaging/scripts/postinstall.sh sets, such as "[all]"
sudo /opt/{{ project_name }}/venv/bin/pip install "{{ pypi_name }}${EXTRAS}==X.Y.Z"
sudo curl -fsSL -o /usr/lib/systemd/system/{{ project_name }}.service \
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{ github_org }}/{{ project_name }}/vX.Y.Z/packaging/{{ project_name }}.service
sudo curl -fsSL -o /etc/{{ project_name }}/env.example \
  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/{{ github_org }}/{{ project_name }}/vX.Y.Z/packaging/env.example
sudo cp /etc/{{ project_name }}/env.example /etc/{{ project_name }}/env
sudo chmod 600 /etc/{{ project_name }}/env
sudo chown {{ project_name }}:{{ project_name }} /var/lib/{{ project_name }}
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
```

Copy the value of `EXTRAS` from `packaging/scripts/postinstall.sh` at the same tag: the package installs those extras, and a manual install without them lacks the features they bring. Then configure and start as above. Python 3.11 or newer with the `venv` module is the one system requirement.

## If it does not start

- `journalctl -u {{ project_name }} -n 50 --no-pager` shows the last records; a configuration error names the variable.
- Run the command as the service user to see the error directly: `sudo -u {{ project_name }} /opt/{{ project_name }}/venv/bin/{{ project_name }} serve --transport http`.
- A permission error on a path outside `/var/lib/{{ project_name }}` is the confinement above: add the drop-in, or move the data.
- `systemd-analyze verify /usr/lib/systemd/system/{{ project_name }}.service` checks the unit after an edit.

